# Stack: across pages, Split items: true breaks a row that does not fit and false moves it whole to the next page.
Document (dev.stxt.pdf):
	Language: en
	Title: Stack · Split items
	Page:
		Paper: A6
		Margin: 12mm
	Styles:
		For element: Paragraph
			Typography:
				Size: 8
				Line height: 11
		For style: column
			Box:
				Padding: 6
				Background: sand
	Section:
		Text: Stack
			Style: topic
		Heading 1:
			Text: Split items
		Paragraph: A row taller than what is left of the page is broken there, each item on its own, with `Split items: true` (the default). With `false` it moves whole to the next page.
			Style: intro
		Paragraph: Below, the first row declares Split items: false and the second one keeps the default. Both are taller than what is left of their page.
		Text: Split items: false
			Style: label
		Stack:
			Split items: false
			Gap: 8
			Item:
				Grow: 1
				Base size: 0
				Block:
					Style: column
					Paragraph: This row does not fit in what is left of its page, so it moves whole to the next page.
					Paragraph: Its items are never broken.
					Paragraph: The space it leaves on the previous page stays empty.
					Paragraph: A row is only broken with false when it does not fit on a whole page either.
					Paragraph: The label above it goes with it, because a label keeps with the next box.
					Paragraph: The previous page keeps only the text above it.
					Paragraph: This row ends here.
			Item:
				Grow: 1
				Base size: 0
				Block:
					Style: column
					Paragraph: The second item goes with it.
		Text: Split items: true
			Style: label
		Stack:
			Gap: 8
			Item:
				Grow: 1
				Base size: 0
				Block:
					Style: column
					Paragraph: The row of this Stack starts under the previous one and continues on the next page, because it is split where the page ends.
					Paragraph: Each item breaks between its own lines.
					Paragraph: The first part of the row fills the page.
					Paragraph: The rest of the row goes on at the top of the next page.
					Paragraph: The background of each item is broken with it.
					Paragraph: Widows and orphans still apply to each paragraph of an item.
					Paragraph: Here the first item is the taller one, so it sets where the row ends.
			Item:
				Grow: 1
				Base size: 0
				Block:
					Style: column
					Paragraph: The second item is broken at the same height as the first one, in parallel.
					Paragraph: Its last lines go on the next page.
					Paragraph: Both items keep their width on the two pages.
